,37018,90,16,21,15170,22,17,24,11035,982,59,9640,65,61,21,284,1124,37018,90,18,17,15170,17,22,92,1124,50853,1124,37018,90,16,21,15170,22,17,24,532,59,921,59,9640,65,61,21,284,1124,37018,90,18,17,1124,50853,220,16,21,15170,17,22,1124,50853,220,22,17,24,532,59,921,59,9640,65,61,21,284,1124,37018,90,20,16,17,15170,16,24,21,23,18,532,59,2533,50,70206,279,19419,510,59,9640,65,61,21,284,1124,2359,7,1124,37018,90,23,15170,17,22,92,1124,1291,29776,17,198,59,2533,50097,279,25031,3704,315,2176,11067,11,582,633,510,59,9640,65,284,1124,5187,1124,37018,90,23,15170,17,22,532,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIf $a$ and $b$ are real numbers, $a^2b^3=\\frac{32}{27}$, and $\\frac{a}{b^3}=\\frac{27}{4}$, what is $a+b$?\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for \\(a + b\\) given the equations \\(a^2b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\) and \\(\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4}\\), we will proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the second equation in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4} \\implies a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\) into the first equation \\(a^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} b^3 \\right)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} \\right)^2 (b^3)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{729}{16} \\right) b^6 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(b^6\\), multiply both sides by \\(\\frac{16}{729}\\):\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32}{27} \\cdot \\frac{16}{729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32 \\cdot 16}{27 \\cdot 729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\nb^6 = \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the sixth root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nb = \\pm \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b4a0dd3c5bf7582709a5372b16ca4dee81643fc2d617fc254b70117f32d0a883"},"seconds":52.742730140686035,"seed":696239751760925442,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for \\(a + b\\) given the equations \\(a^2b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\) and \\(\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4}\\), we will follow a systematic approach.\n\nFirst, rewrite the second equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4} \\implies a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\) into the first equation \\(a^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} b^3 \\right)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} \\right)^2 (b^3)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{729}{16} \\right) b^6 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(b^6\\), multiply both sides by \\(\\frac{16}{729}\\):\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32}{27} \\cdot \\frac{16}{729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32 \\cdot 16}{27 \\cdot 729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\nTaking the sixth root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nb = \\left( \\frac{512}{19683} \\right)^{1/6}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\left( \\frac{2^9}{3^9} \\right)^{1/6}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\left( \\frac{2^3}{3^3} \\right)^{1/2}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^{1/2}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(b = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}\\) back into \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\):\n\\[\nb^3 = \\left( \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9} \\right)^3 = \\frac{8 \\cdot 6\\sqrt{6}}{729} = \\frac{48\\sqrt{6}}{729} = \\frac{16\\sqrt{6}}{243}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot \\frac{16\\sqrt{6}}{243} = \\frac{27 \\cdot 16\\sqrt{6}}{4 \\cdot 243} = \\frac{432\\sqrt{6}}{972} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{9}\n\\]\n\nFinally, compute \\(a + b\\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{9} + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9} = \\frac{6\\sqrt{6}}{9} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve for \\(a + b\\) given the equations \\(a^2b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\) and \\(\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4}\\), we will follow a systematic approach.\n\nFirst, rewrite the second equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4} \\implies a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\) into the first equation \\(a^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} b^3 \\right)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} \\right)^2 (b^3)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{729}{16} \\right) b^6 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(b^6\\), multiply both sides by \\(\\frac{16}{729}\\):\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32}{27} \\cdot \\frac{16}{729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32 \\cdot 16}{27 \\cdot 729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\nTaking the sixth root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nb = \\left( \\frac{512}{19683} \\right)^{1/6}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\left( \\frac{2^9}{3^9} \\right)^{1/6}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\left( \\frac{2^3}{3^3} \\right)^{1/2}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^{1/2}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(b = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}\\) back into \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\):\n\\[\nb^3 = \\left( \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9} \\right)^3 = \\frac{8 \\cdot 6\\sqrt{6}}{729} = \\frac{48\\sqrt{6}}{729} = \\frac{16\\sqrt{6}}{243}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot \\frac{16\\sqrt{6}}{243} = \\frac{27 \\cdot 16\\sqrt{6}}{4 \\cdot 243} = \\frac{432\\sqrt{6}}{972} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{9}\n\\]\n\nFinally, compute \\(a + b\\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{9} + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9} = \\frac{6\\sqrt{6}}{9} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}}\n\\]","id":"algebra:912"}
